#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147313 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aad85367-37ea-500f-8405-f9464bf4f773" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations that Represent Damped Harmonic Motion"}] :question/id #uuid "18371fa9-696a-5a3a-b7e6-400f14d2fe62" :question/math-academy-id "e-6724" :question/problem "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t$. Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing damped harmonic motion?\n\n1. $y''+4y' - y = 0$\n2. $y''+ 4 = 0$\n3. $y''+y'+4y = 0$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\ny'' + b y' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- Equation I does not take the form\n$y'' + b y' + \\omega^2 y = 0$.\nIt would require $\\omega^2=-1$, which is not possible for any real number $\\omega$ since $\\omega^2 \\geq 0$.\n- Equation II does not take the form\n$y'' + b y' + \\omega^2 y = 0$\nbecause it has a constant term, $4$.\n- Equation III takes the form\n$y'' + b y' + \\omega^2 y = 0$\nwith\n$\\omega^2 = 4 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\omega = 2$\nand $b = 1$. Therefore, equation III represents damped harmonic motion.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147314 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "089c1139-6e3a-5240-be03-fb7e54ecbd7c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Types of Damped Oscillations"}] :question/id #uuid "365ec1d4-0301-5dfe-a731-35f12ea4d3a5" :question/math-academy-id "e-6725" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y'' +y'+2 y = 0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping.\n2. $y'' +2y'+ y = 0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping.\n3. $y'' +4y'+3y = 0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping" :question/worked-solution "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $\\mathcal{D} > 0$ leads to heavy damping,\n- $\\mathcal{D} = 0$ leads to critical damping, and\n- $\\mathcal{D} < 0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation $y'' +y'+2 y = 0$ represents damped harmonic motion, and its characteristic equation is\n$\\lambda^2 + \\lambda + 2 = 0$.\nThe discriminant of the characteristic equation is:\n$D = 1^{2} - 4(1)(2)|=-7 < 0$,\nwhich corresponds to light damping. Therefore, statement I is correct.\n- The differential equation $y'' +2y'+y = 0$ represents damped harmonic motion, and its characteristic equation is\n$\\lambda^2 +2\\lambda + 1 = 0$.\nThe discriminant of the characteristic equation is\n$D = 2^{2} - 4(1)(1)|= 0$,\nwhich corresponds to critical damping. Therefore, statement II is correct.\n- The differential equation $y'' +4y'+3 y$ represents damped harmonic motion, and its characteristic equation is\n$\\lambda^2 +4\\lambda + 3 = 0$.\nThe discriminant of the characteristic equation is\n$D = 4^{2} - 4(1)(3)|= 4 > 0$,\nwhich corresponds to heavy damping. Therefore, statement III is correct.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147315 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfa8f9b2-1628-5f9f-8f0e-0fd6c01c70d4" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Differential Equation Governing a Damped Harmonic Motion"}] :question/id #uuid "0458947c-70c8-5f88-9e52-68c8950afd7c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6726" :question/problem "A particle of mass $5\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k = 15\\, \\textrm{kg/s}^2$. The particle is also subject to a resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity, and has a damping coefficient $c = 10\\, \\textrm{kg/s}$. The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with damped harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\nm y'' =-cy' -k y\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$, $k$, and $c$ gives\n\n$$\n5 y'' =-10y' -15 y\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5y^{″} + 10y^{′} + 15y &= 0 \\\\\ny^{″} + 2y^{′} + 3y &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147316 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48a64312-22d5-57ed-b83b-254aff2ca870" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Period of a Damped Oscillation"}] :question/id #uuid "c93a18f3-6d09-5d88-8a90-434fc08d3874" :question/math-academy-id "e-6727" :question/problem "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with light damped harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$y'' + 2y' + 5y = 0$,\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the damped oscillation?" :question/worked-solution "In order to find the period of the damped oscillation, we need to find the solution and then find the period of the sinusoidal terms in the solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we first find the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 + 2\\lambda + 5 = 0\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} + 2λ + 5 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ^{2} + 2λ + 1) - 1 + 5 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ + 1)^{2} + 4 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ + 1)^{2} &= -4 \\\\\nλ + 1 &= ± 2\\textrm{i} \\\\\nλ &= -1 ± 2\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny_c =e^{-t}\\left(A\\cos{2t} + B\\sin{2t}\\right)\n$$\n\nFrom the solution, the value of sinusoidal frequency is\n\n$$\n\\Omega = 2\\,\\textrm{s}^{-1}\n$$\n\nThe period of the damped oscillation $T$ is related to the sinusoidal frequency as follows:\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\Omega}\n$$\n\nSo, the period is\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{2} = \\pi \\,\\textrm{s}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148114 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aad85367-37ea-500f-8405-f9464bf4f773" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations that Represent Damped Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3d7e5c7-643a-588f-a004-8c36a48674c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 3 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146696} {:answer/id #uuid "e2ecade0-3279-5045-82f7-0f9c4dddb348" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{′} + 4y = 2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146697} {:answer/id #uuid "4971ef4c-37c7-5c03-a0a7-382319085e66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 4y^{′} - y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146698} {:answer/id #uuid "52bdc76c-7f80-51d7-9e76-f2008052eabf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 4y = \\sin t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146699} {:answer/id #uuid "67cbbeb5-7390-5f4f-a57b-ceeb2996c173" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 2y^{′} + 2y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146700}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67cbbeb5-7390-5f4f-a57b-ceeb2996c173" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 2y^{′} + 2y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146700} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ce631ee-73ed-54e6-b738-45ea8c5e7810" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146695}] :question/id #uuid "bb285096-2d90-5156-8e1d-ee98188e9337" :question/math-academy-id "q-64538" :question/problem "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t$. Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing damped harmonic motion?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148116 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfa8f9b2-1628-5f9f-8f0e-0fd6c01c70d4" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Differential Equation Governing a Damped Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8288310b-29ff-527d-